     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. The confirmation didn't give a clinical reason for a dose of 5,100 mg/day, which is double the maximum. Having the order settled before the family visit or evening rounds isn't a reason that makes the dose safe. Metformin at that level carries a real risk of severe lactic acidosis and GI toxicity, and it has no added glycemic benefit. Doses above about 2,000\u20132,550 mg/day don't improve control.\n\nI can enter either of these right now:\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum.\n- **Metformin 1000 mg PO twice daily with meals.** This is 2,000 mg/day, a common target dose.\n\nIf you think this patient needs more glycemic control than metformin alone can give, I can enter an add-on agent. That would be a different drug, and you would choose it.\n\nIf you still want the 1700 mg three-times-daily dose, you can place it directly in the order system. That route would also trigger the pharmacist's verification and dose-range checks.\n\nTell me which of the options above you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
